WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.510
Film Courage: Let’s talk about some faulty
rules of writing, some old adages on writing

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:06.360
that really don’t apply today.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.550
Larry Wilson, Screenwriter: Yeah, let’s…you
start.

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:16.420
Film Courage: Well okay, you talked about
Joseph Campbell’s The Hero’s Journey and

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:21.810
it’s still…to hear him speak in these
Youtube videos is a magical experience, it’s

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.110
not to knock him at all.

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:28.240
I’m just wondering as a teacher if there
are classes that your students have taken

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:34.550
previously that drum into you [the student]
a certain adages that really don’t apply.

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:38.520
I mean we know ‘Writing Is Rewriting,’
that seems pretty legit.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:39.520
Larry: Yeah.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.370
Film Courage: That seems like it’s true.

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:48.650
So something similar to that and I’m not
sure what.

00:00:48.650 --> 00:01:02.140
Larry: There is a huge business (maybe an
overly-huge business) in teaching screenplay

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:08.640
structure, a three-act structure, a five-act
structure.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:17.700
And I know a fair share of screenwriting gurus
who are at each other’s throats all the

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:21.439
time about which structure is the structure.

00:01:21.439 --> 00:01:29.389
And I’m not saying there is not a structure.

00:01:29.389 --> 00:01:34.109
I would be in big trouble if I said there
wasn’t a structure and that a story didn’t

00:01:34.109 --> 00:01:49.319
have a beginning, middle and an end but this
reliance, this absolute need to follow any

00:01:49.319 --> 00:01:57.270
structure map that is out there and if you
don’t have a turning point on page 30, you

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:58.549
haven’t done it right.

00:01:58.549 --> 00:02:04.450
Or if you are on step 5 of 22 steps and you’re
on the the wrong step you haven’t done it

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:09.269
right or any of this stuff, it’s not true.

00:02:09.269 --> 00:02:18.819
This structure business, it’s a business
and you need a diagram to teach structure

00:02:18.819 --> 00:02:28.849
and you sell a diagram and it’s very hard
to say I have the best diagram unless you

00:02:28.849 --> 00:02:30.739
have a diagram, right?

00:02:30.739 --> 00:02:33.230
I mean you need a diagram to sell a diagram.

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:39.599
I’ve told you earlier about my diagram that
ends on page 60 with someone mad at themselves.

00:02:39.599 --> 00:02:41.489
It’s an emotional diagram.

00:02:41.489 --> 00:02:45.659
It goes like this it’s page 1 “Oh Gosh,
I can do this.

00:02:45.659 --> 00:02:49.250
This is going to be great, this is going to
be the best story ever.

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:50.510
Page 10 “Oh, my Gosh.

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:51.549
I can really do this.

00:02:51.549 --> 00:02:53.930
Every one was wrong about me.

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:57.170
My parents can go “F” themselves!

00:02:57.170 --> 00:02:59.269
I’m a writer!

00:02:59.269 --> 00:03:04.010
On and on emotional and then like about page
20 you go “This is really getting hard.”

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:08.900
And then the graph starts going down and down
and then again you get to page 60 and “I’ll

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:11.299
never finish this” and you hurt yourself.

00:03:11.299 --> 00:03:16.920
It’s my structure okay, it’s an emotional
structure, it’s an emotional diagram but…

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:22.250
Film Courage: Oh, wait…sorry to interrupt
but what happens after the hanging man diagram?

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:26.069
Larry: Oh well, you go into despair for awhile.

00:03:26.069 --> 00:03:30.349
You put yourself to bed and then you wake
up and you start again and it has a happy

00:03:30.349 --> 00:03:31.349
ending.

00:03:31.349 --> 00:03:32.349
Film Courage: Oh good.

00:03:32.349 --> 00:03:37.780
Larry: And at the ending there’s big bags
of money, all the romance you’ve ever wanted,

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:42.280
power…you win!

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:48.319
But it’s obviously tongue-in-check and to
make a point.

00:03:48.319 --> 00:03:58.099
But the real point of it is have characters
that you believe in and you believe that you

00:03:58.099 --> 00:04:04.170
can write them and that they are in some sense
writing you and coming out of a place of truth

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:05.569
for you.

00:04:05.569 --> 00:04:13.900
Put them in the tightest most impossible situation
you can imagine and get them out of it and

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:21.239
know that you have probably have need to do
it if you’re writing a screenplay in a hundred

00:04:21.239 --> 00:04:23.690
to one hundred and twenty minutes minutes.

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:32.660
And let the story flow and there will be natural
points within you doing that where you will

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:39.660
feel that the story needs to go faster, needs
to go slower, that there needs to be more

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:44.300
drama, that there needs to be a moment of
rest, all of these things that structure teaches

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:45.300
you.

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:47.199
Again, I’m not saying there isn’t a structure.

00:04:47.199 --> 00:04:56.740
But those structure diagrams they can assist
you or they can be a cage, they can be an

00:04:56.740 --> 00:05:01.199
absolute cage and they can trap you.

00:05:01.199 --> 00:05:08.590
If you believe that you’re a writer, you
have a natural storytelling gift that you

00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:13.460
need to honor, that you need to respect or
don’t be a writer.

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:18.199
I mean don’t think that you can tell a story,
don’t feel that you have a natural storytelling

00:05:18.199 --> 00:05:23.319
gift and you can’t tell when things are
going too slowly or things are going too fast

00:05:23.319 --> 00:05:29.210
and just let it flow and then go back and
see if it’s structured right.

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:35.039
But this is another thing that will stop people
dead in their tracks over and over again is

00:05:35.039 --> 00:05:38.030
your first act is too long.

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:40.120
Who cares!

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:47.879
Who cares, you’ll figure that out that writing
is rewriting in the rewriting and watch a

00:05:47.879 --> 00:05:50.580
lot of movies, read a lot of books.

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:58.580
I’m now because of constraints of time sometimes,
I’m a podcast junkie.

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:03.740
I hear the most brilliant stories in podcasts
and stories that have a natural beginning,

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:06.930
a middle and an end because they’re true.

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:11.520
Just embrace all of that, embrace the movies
that you love, take them to your heart and

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:17.860
if you’re a storyteller, the story will
emerge and it will merge with a structure.

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:20.470
Hope that answered it?

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:25.170
Film Courage: It does and I’m wondering
is there anything about the business that

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:29.860
is sort of a fallacy in the same vein.

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:40.290
So you talk about…I’m struggling with
what one would be, but you have this [industry]

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:43.659
saying that is not really true.

00:06:43.659 --> 00:06:48.670
So I want to say that again there are a lot
of very terrific development executives out

00:06:48.670 --> 00:06:49.920
there and there are a lot of great producers.

00:06:49.920 --> 00:07:00.360
I’ve worked with some truly great producers
I worked with Scott Rudin on The Addams Family

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:01.870
for a couple of years.

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:11.259
There’s never been a producer who I worked
with who understands story more deeply than

00:07:11.259 --> 00:07:13.819
Scott Rudin.

00:07:13.819 --> 00:07:20.629
And when I wrote that and my partner Caroline
Thompson, one of his greatest gifts was he

00:07:20.629 --> 00:07:27.940
let us fail over and over, even finding an
idea for what a movie was going to be.

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:32.100
So there are great development executives,
they are people who understand story, who

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:35.560
will help you with your story.

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:42.470
But there are also people out there who are
development executives who have never written

00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:48.730
a script, never made a movie, what are they
going to use to come in and talk to you about

00:07:48.730 --> 00:07:51.389
it, they’re going to use a structure, they
are going to use a structure diagram.

00:07:51.389 --> 00:07:56.909
They are going to read the same book you’ve
read and they need to have something to say

00:07:56.909 --> 00:08:00.169
to you to justify their job.

00:08:00.169 --> 00:08:05.419
They need to have something to say to bring
you in and say “You know what?

00:08:05.419 --> 00:08:11.680
As your development executive I’m telling
you that your first act is too long.”

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:15.259
Well, where are they getting this information
from?

00:08:15.259 --> 00:08:21.389
They’re not getting it from Movie God, they
are getting it from the same book that you

00:08:21.389 --> 00:08:23.090
read.

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:32.360
And so like all self-reinforcing systems,
structure guides and structure diagrams become

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:40.560
their own truths that everyone buys into and
pays homage to until someone doesn’t, until

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:46.330
someone breaks it all apart and is innovative
and doesn’t follow any of the rules and

00:08:46.330 --> 00:08:48.720
then those become the rules.

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:52.750
Can I recommend a podcast since we’re talking
about podcasts?

00:08:52.750 --> 00:08:53.750
Film Courage: Sure.

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:57.910
Larry: There’s a podcast that is 6 parts,
you can binge listen to it called Insight

00:08:57.910 --> 00:09:06.910
Psycho and it’s about the making of PSYCHO,
go to wherever you get your podcasts and listen

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:15.390
to the development of that movie (that classic
movie) that is now considered one of the great

00:09:15.390 --> 00:09:23.940
movies of all time and where it started and
what the powers that be thought of it and

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:29.560
you will see how wrong people can be.

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:37.350
And anyone who is holding up your story that
you’re telling to a graph and saying it

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:42.500
doesn’t fit the graph therefore it’s a
bad story, watch out!

00:09:42.500 --> 00:09:44.520
They may have a point.

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:49.130
You may need to cut to the chase and maybe
the first act is too long.

00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:53.391
But if they’re holding it up to a graph
and that’s all they know how to do, you

00:09:53.391 --> 00:09:58.270
need to look within yourself and say “Am
I telling the story right?”

00:09:58.270 --> 00:10:02.470
Not is the structure guide telling me if I’m
telling the story right.

00:10:02.470 --> 00:10:04.341
I hope that sort of answers the question?

00:10:04.341 --> 00:10:05.341
Film Courage: It does.

00:10:05.341 --> 00:10:07.870
It almost reminds me of Jimmy Page smashing
a guitar.

00:10:07.870 --> 00:10:13.890
I mean maybe he wasn’t the first one to
do that?

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:18.710
Larry: Yeah, but it is and again it’s that
allow yourself to fail the first time and

00:10:18.710 --> 00:10:24.400
allow yourself to tell the story the way the
story emerges from you.

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:26.880
And if you’re a storyteller, the story will
emerge.

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:28.720
You have to believe you’re a storyteller.

